WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.000
The United States is

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.350
the most refrigerated country in the world.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.980
We have more than a hundred million cubic meters

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.120
of thermal control.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.320
That means nothing to me. But once I did the math...

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:18.320
That is actually not so much smaller than the Arctic.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.780
So basically we built a third Pole

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.720
for our food to live in. Which is incredible.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.540
And then, all these separate refrigerated spaces

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.380
are connected by a cold chain.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.240
So you end up with this sort of seamless

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.840
monument of engineered winter.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.880
And the thing that happened to me is

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.060
I realized, I'd been writing about food for
a decade at this point

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.260
when I first turned my attention to the cold chain.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.700
And I realized... I had visited farms, and

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.500
I had visited factories,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.100
and obviously I'd talked to chefs, and I had I'd even

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.280
been to the grocery store on occasion.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.820
But I had never been inside a refrigerated warehouse.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.740
And I'm gonna guess most of you haven't either.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.240
And here we are in the Bay Area,

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.920
which is home of farm-to-table.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.620
But that "to" word... it goes via

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.480
the refrigerated warehouse.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.090
Like in between farm and table

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.300
is a giant cold space that nobody thinks about.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.640
So I decided to explore it.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.240
And I partnered with

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.000
the Center for Land Use Interpretation (CLUI)

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.940
which, for those of you haven't heard of it,

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.520
you are in for a treat when you Google it later.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.880
But we mapped the American "cryosphere."

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.680
And then I still wasn't done.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.420
So then I wrote a piece

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.980
for the New York Times Magazine that took me to China

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.060
to explore their artificial cryosphere.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.380
And then I still wasn't done.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:02.120
So I decided to sell a book on this topic

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.640
and research that and write that

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.220
and that is still not done

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.120
but that's a whole other story.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.560
So I have gone deep into this artificial cryosphere

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:19.240
and my goal tonight is: let's go on a quick spin

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.580
through this space past present future.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.180
I hope you wrapped up warmly.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.400
We are stepping inside.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.260
And I want to start this thing right...

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.420
with a banquet.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:37.840
This in its time was the most talked about meal

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.480
in the United States.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:44.240
The organizers had originally booked a small room.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.200
They had to upgrade to the largest

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.740
banqueting hall in Chicago.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:51.160
The guest list kept expanding.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.880
A member of Congress decided they wanted to come.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.660
Then other members of Congress

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.020
decided they wanted to come.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.880
People were flooding this...

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.320
and so what is so special about this meal you ask?

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:07.080
Well this was the world's first

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.940
cold storage banquet.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.160
Everything on the menu had been stored

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.720
under refrigeration.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.940
Except I should point out for the olives

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:23.060
in the dry martini. Not refrigerated.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.800
So this took place on Monday, October 23rd, 1911.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.660
And there's a thousand people in the room.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:36.800
White linen... full table service... the crystal is out.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.900
The result was 2 hours of what industry journal

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.760
The Egg Reporter called "unalloyed pleasure."

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.360
And what is amazing about it

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.680
is that everything on the menu

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.680
had spent at least 6 months

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.080
and up to a year in cold storage.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.880
And what I love about this menu too is,

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.720
again here in the Bay Area,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.080
we are used to seeing who grew

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.600
our... this is Mary's chicken,

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:11.160
or this particular heirloom variety of tomato.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.740
Do we ever see what cold storage it was in?

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:14.760
We do not.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.500
Here we have Booth's Cold Storage, proud

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.160
the last address of that salmon.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:32.180
And the introduction to the meal explained

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.240
your turkey that's eleven months old

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.980
your eggs (seven months)

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:46.540
the President of the National Poultry, Butter and Egg Association, who was giving this introduction

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:49.880
said "Your capon received its summons

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:54.980
to the great unknown last Valentine's Day,"

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.260
The pie, even the pie: cold stored flour,

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.580
butter churned in June. Apples, and this is October,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.780
so they they could have had fresh. But, no.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:08.460
They'd been in storage alongside the salmon 
at Booth's for a year.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.340
The head chef of the hotel

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.620
where this was being served, a man called Lucien, said

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:20.240
"There has never been a better meal
served at this hotel."

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.860
The San Antonio Express reporter said

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.000
"Nothing was quite fresh."

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.020
The president of the Poultrymen's Association

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:42.080
said "This is edible proof that cold stored foods

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:45.260
are not only good they're better

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:47.540
than the ones that you would eat fresh."

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:48.760
And the congressman

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.980
who was called upon to speak

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.460
agreed and said "Yes,

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:55.900
I really believe that there is more flavor

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.654
to cold storage poultry than the kind 
that is advertised as freshly killed."

00:06:00.654 --> 00:06:02.860
And he said "I'm going back to Washington.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.460
I'm telling the folks: cold storage is where it's at."

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:10.700
So this kind of lunch became a national trend.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.560
So L.A. held the next one. Then there was one in Detroit.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:14.720
There was one in Philly.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.040
2 years later there was another one in
Chicago

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.220
with literally thousands of people,

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.220
and a special three-page souvenir brochure

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.200
with not only where the food had been stored.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.960
But also the temperature and relative humidity

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.800
of where it had been stored.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:37.220
So it became a trend. And this is
barely more than a century ago.

